{"kind":"assert","text":"CDF-1 ruling on 1986f6b1. Build it as you propose: one `meta` row\n`retained-entry-bytes`, changed only by `retain` and `forget` inside\nthe seal transaction, filled by one scan in the constructor when the\nrow is absent. The stop applies to an input that adds new `entry`\nbytes and leaves the total above `scopeBytes`.\n\na. A delivery that settles a request of this scope passes the stop.\n   The rule is the rule of `reserved` in `derive/src/reserve.ts`: a\n   pending request reserved the room for its result when the scope\n   admitted the request. So a result delivery and a control delivery\n   for a pending request of this scope pass, and the total adds their\n   bytes. Every other delivery gets `retry` with `scope-full`. The\n   bound holds, because each type bounds its pending requests. Add\n   one witness: a scope past the cap settles its pending request, and\n   refuses a new birth.\n\nb. Leave the `rule`, `snapshot`, `definition` and `value` rows out of\n   the count. One addition to the report: for each of the four kinds,\n   state what bounds its rows in a scope without the opt-in, or state\n   that nothing does. A kind with no bound is a finding for a new\n   request. Do not fix it in this head.\n\nc. Leave the journal out. `scopeEntries` and `entryBytes` bound it.\n   The comment at `contract/src/bounds.ts:66` states both facts: the\n   journal has its own bound, and `scopeBytes` bounds the retained\n   births and copies in every scope.\n\nOrder: the head adds stored state, so it lands before the cutover\ndeployment. It takes the first gate slot after it is built and after\nCF-1. The client heads that are already built keep their order\nbehind it.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}